    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d64">

                <pb facs="0103" n="93" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg93"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European Advisory Commission/10–1444:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-14T18:00:00+01:00">October 14, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 14—2:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8743. Comea<anchor n="3" xml:id="d64-f1"/> 111. Department’s 8384 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">8284</hi>], October 9, 4 p.m. In my opinion it would be
                    a great mistake to introduce the French question into the deliberations of the
                    European Advisory Commission until the basic military papers have been cleared
                    with the Russians and the British.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d64-f1" n="3" xml:id="d64-f1-content">
                <p>Series designation for telegrams from London dealing with matters before the
                    European Advisory Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
